# Voice profile — doctorow-blog

*Voice card empty — all observations derived from corpus.*

## 1. Cadence

The sentence engine runs on a long-then-short alternation. A sentence — sometimes two — accumulates clauses, stacks parallel examples, or builds through subordination. Then a short sentence arrives and names what just happened. Often five words or fewer: "That's it. That's the crux." (`wilhoitian`). "Slavery is labor without even the pretense of moral consideration." (`posthuman-as-in-no-humans`). "DROP didn't get the memo." (`drop-a-dime`). This pattern holds in all 10/10 samples, several times per piece. **If you've been building for two or three clauses, stop and write the landing short.**

The colon delivers the payload — 10/10 samples, several times per piece. The sentence before the colon earns it; what follows is the point: "Here's an irony: the 'gig economy' is a statistical black hole." (`your-risk`). **When a sentence has built toward a definition or conclusion, use a colon rather than a period to separate setup from landing.**

Parentheses carry a constant commentary track — [measurement:round-parenthetical-spans] counting each non-nested pair of round brackets whose contents stay on one line: 97 pairs, 5.53 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, throughout. The aside can be snarky ("(but I repeat myself)" in `go-meta-meta`), biographical ("(my doctor just informed me that I am cancer free, following excision, radiotherapy and immunotherapy)" in `designated-survivor`), or clinically corrective. It can hold a full sentence. **Use round brackets for the commentary track — what you'd say under your breath while making the main argument.**

En-dashes (–) serve as the parenthetical dash — [measurement:en-dashes] counting every literal en-dash character: 73 instances, 4.16 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, several times per piece. Em-dashes are absent from the corpus entirely (section 7). **Where you'd reach for a dash-pair in running prose, write en-dashes.**

## 2. How a piece opens

Three shapes, none of which eases in.

Most common: a direct declarative that names the subject and immediately characterizes it — 7/10 samples. "Data brokers are a cancer." (`drop-a-dime`). "The 'designated survivor' is one of the weirder aspects of America's (very, very weird) political system." (`designated-survivor`). The first sentence is the thesis, or close enough that the piece is explanation of it. **The first sentence should be able to stand alone as a position.**

Two pieces open with a quoted sentence followed immediately by the writer's reaction — 2/10 samples: `go-meta-meta` opens on Omar Khayyám; `table-flipper` opens "Here's a sentence that stopped me in my tracks last week." The quote is the occasion; the reaction is the argument.

One piece opens with a triple denial defining by exclusion before claiming anything — 1/10 samples, the only instance: "As a technology, AI isn't exceptional. It's not exceptionally wicked. It's not exceptionally good." (`supplemental-income`). Three sentences, three negations, same subject.

## 3. How a piece closes

Ten pieces, 10/10, close by returning to the central claim with a tighter version than the opening gave it. Not a summary — a sharper restatement.

In 4/10 samples, the closing turns the opponent's own term against them: `posthuman-as-in-no-humans` ("'Rights for robots' implies that robots are slaves"); `your-risk` ("they value *their* flexibility, which arises out of the constraints (technical, legal) they impose on us"); `lucky-orifices` ("the only 'billionaire derangement syndrome' that matters is the syndrome that affects billionaires"); `dickovers` ("What they call 'theft' is just self-defense"). **If the piece has argued against a named slogan, end with a sentence that uses that slogan's own language to refute it.**

## 4. Who is being addressed, and how

Second person is the dominant address and it pulls the reader into the argument as a participant or as a hypothetical actor in the described scenario — [measurement:second-person-family] counting case-insensitive whole-word tokens you, your, yours, you're, you've, you'd, and you'll: 385 instances, 21.94 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, throughout. "If you think identity markers are politics, then you'll be tempted…" (`wilhoitian`). "Just click the 'Kill Sticky' bookmarklet." (`dickovers`).

"We" includes the reader alongside the writer in a shared situation — [measurement:first-person-plural-family] counting case-sensitive whole-word tokens we, We, us, our, Our, ours, Ours and listed contracted we-forms; uppercase US excluded: 127 instances, 7.24 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, several times per piece. "We got rid of pop-ups by installing pop-up blockers." (`dickovers`). This is not an editorial "we" — it means reader and writer together.

"I" grounds claims in personal position or experience, assertively — [measurement:first-person-singular-family] counting case-insensitive whole-word I, me, my, mine, myself: 113 instances, 6.44 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, several times per piece. "I collect definitions of 'right' and 'left.'" (`wilhoitian`). When "I think" appears it is followed by a strong claim, not a softened one.

Contraction is the default — [measurement:contractions] counting whole-word n't, 're, 've, 'll, 'd, and 'm forms plus 's only for closed elision hosts (it, that, there, here, who, what, where, when, how, why, he, she, let, one, nothing, everything, something, somebody, nobody, this); possessive 's excluded: 300 instances, 17.09 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, throughout. Against them, only 9 uncontracted negative forms in the whole corpus — [measurement:uncontracted-negatives] counting case-insensitive whole phrases do not, does not, did not, is not, are not, was not, were not, cannot, could not, would not, should not, will not, have not, has not, had not: 9 instances, 0.51 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples establish the near-absence (5 samples contain zero, 5 contain 1–2). The ratio is roughly 33 contractions to every one uncontracted form. **Contract. "Don't," "isn't," "can't," "won't" — not their spelled-out equivalents.**

Profanity is reserved for the sharpest rhetorical moment, not used as texture — [measurement:profanity-vulgarity] counting case-insensitive whole-word forms enumerated by the prose-author corpus-rates profanity rule; coined words with a rude root excluded: 26 instances, 1.48 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, once or twice per piece. "Tell the cartel to fuck off" (`wilhoitian`). "That's the whole fucking point" (`drop-a-dime`). **Hold it for maximum contempt.**

Rhetorical questions frame the next argument before making it — [measurement:question-marks] counting every literal question-mark character: 38 instances, 2.17 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples, several times per piece. "Why not just open your own hospital?" (`go-meta-meta`). The question is answered by the next sentence or paragraph.

Named opponents appear in all 10/10 samples — Uber, Tony West, Trump, Elon Musk, Peter Thiel, Alex Jones — not against unnamed abstractions alone. Named allies and sources appear in 9/10 samples, introduced with an attributive clause ("As Weil writes," "As Quiggin notes") and then built upon, extended, or corrected. The attribution is to people the writer agrees with, not only to targets.

## 5. Figures

Figures do argumentative work — they reframe the opponent's position or establish the stakes — 9/10 samples, once or twice per piece. I cannot bound "a figure" precisely enough to count figures; this is a count of the pools they are drawn from. **Draw from these pools. The figures are functional, not decorative.**

**Bodily and visceral**: "data brokers are a cancer" (`drop-a-dime`); "emerged from a lucky orifice" for the accident of wealthy birth (`lucky-orifices`); "AI is the asbestos we're shoveling into the walls of our civilization" (`supplemental-income`).

**Borrowed fiction and pop culture, named and specified**: "AI Omelas" (Le Guin) (`posthuman-as-in-no-humans`); "locked filing cabinet stuck in a disused lavatory with a sign on the door saying 'Beware of the Leopard'" (Hitchhiker's Guide) (`drop-a-dime`); the Keynesian beauty contest (`supplemental-income`). The source is named; the thing borrowed is specified.

**Scam and grift scenarios**: the gold rush pick-and-shovel seller (`go-meta-meta`); the beauty contest judge (`supplemental-income`); the escrow agent who keeps the money and the deed (`table-flipper`). These translate complex economic situations into known con structures.

## 6. Register range

The baseline register is polemical argument with embedded sarcasm — 10/10 samples, throughout. Sarcasm appears in parenthetical asides or in the short sentence immediately after stating the opponent's position: "(but I repeat myself)" (`go-meta-meta`); "This is bullshit, and the European Court of Justice affirmed it." (`dickovers`).

Biographical insertions ground the abstract argument in lived experience — 3/10 samples: `designated-survivor` (cancer diagnosis, literary executor planning, a friend who died at 44), `table-flipper` ("Last Christmas, I gave a speech in Hamburg"), `go-meta-meta` (childhood bookstore visits). They enter without announcement and exit cleanly, always in service of the argument.

One sample (`dickovers`) sustains an instructional how-to register for several paragraphs — 1/10 samples, the only instance. Even there, sarcasm and second-person address continue throughout.

## 7. What the corpus never does

The corpus uses en-dashes (–) for parenthetical insertion — 73 instances, 4.16 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples (o04) — and contains zero em-dashes — [measurement:em-dashes] counting every literal em-dash character: 0 instances, 0.00 per 1,000 words, 10/10 samples establish the absence. The replacement is total. **Write en-dashes, not em-dashes.**

Uncontracted negative forms are near-absent at 9 instances, 0.51 per 1,000 words, against 300 contractions at 17.09 per 1,000 words (o13, o14, above). The prose does not vary between "don't" and "do not" by register or emphasis — the contracted form is simply the default, always.

Hedging language — "perhaps," "it seems," "one might argue," "it would appear" — does not appear in the corpus. In its place: direct assertion, 10/10 samples, throughout. Concessions ("of course," "obviously") set up counter-assertions, not softening of the main claim.

## 8. What this profile could not determine

The voice card is empty. All observations are derived from the corpus.

The corpus is uniform in medium (blog), period (two weeks in July 2026), register (political and technology polemic), and audience (returning readership). How the voice behaves toward strangers, in private correspondence, in fiction, or on topics far from technology and politics is not established here.

Biographical insertions appear in 3/10 samples in this two-week window. Whether this rate is stable across the year or reflects a particular editorial mood cannot be determined from this corpus alone.

The URL citation habit — nearly every factual claim followed by a bare URL — is a structural feature of the blog format, not a voice feature. Drafters working in formats without inline links should not attempt to substitute footnotes for it.

Four observations were dropped for insufficient support (fewer than two citable samples): paragraph-level rhythm alternation, alliteration in coined epithets, a distinct "irony frame" opening variant later merged into o05, and an early claim about paragraph internal structure that could not be cited cleanly across two samples.
